MultiModal Medical Imaging Viewer - Technical Documentation & Development Reference
Project Overview
Component Name: MultiModalViewer.jsx
Date Developed: January 2025
Developer: USRad Development Team
Purpose: Interactive medical imaging viewer for educational and demonstration purposes
Current Implementation (v1.0)
Core Features

MRI Viewer (Fully Implemented)

Displays 24 T1-weighted axial brain MRI slices
Real patient data from TCIA DFCI-BCH-BWH Pediatric HGG Dataset
Interactive controls: brightness, contrast, zoom, pan
Slice navigation: scroll wheel, drag, arrow buttons
Animation playback with adjustable speed (0.075s - 0.5s per slice)
Anatomically accurate labels for each slice level


Placeholder Modalities

CT, Ultrasound, PET: "Coming Soon" modal
Infrastructure ready for expansion



Technical Architecture
Data Structure
javascriptmodalityConfig = {
  mri: {
    name: 'MRI',
    fullName: 'Magnetic Resonance Imaging',
    totalSlices: 24,
    imagePath: '/mri/patient_001/',
    sequenceType: 'T1-weighted',
    scanInfo: {
      field: '3T',
      sliceThickness: '5mm',
      acquisition: 'Axial',
      source: 'TCIA DFCI-BCH-BWH',
      status: 'Pediatric HGG Dataset'
    }
  }
}
Image Format & Naming Convention

Format: WebP (optimized for web)
Naming: slice_XXX.webp (zero-padded, 3 digits)
Location: /public/mri/patient_001/
Range: slice_000.webp through slice_024.webp

Key Components

Canvas-based rendering for performance
React hooks for state management
Responsive design with Tailwind CSS
Accessibility features included

Future Expansion Guide
Adding CT Modality
1. Image Preparation
Directory: /public/ct/patient_001/
Format: slice_XXX.webp
Typical range: 100-300 slices (depending on coverage)
Window/Level presets needed:
- Bone: W:2000 L:500
- Soft tissue: W:400 L:40
- Lung: W:1500 L:-600
2. Code Modifications
javascriptct: {
  name: 'CT',
  fullName: 'Computed Tomography',
  totalSlices: [ACTUAL_COUNT],
  imagePath: '/ct/patient_001/',
  scanInfo: {
    kvp: '120',
    mAs: 'Auto',
    sliceThickness: '1mm',
    reconstruction: 'Standard',
    contrast: 'Non-contrast' // or 'IV contrast'
  },
  windowPresets: [
    { name: 'Bone', window: 2000, level: 500 },
    { name: 'Soft Tissue', window: 400, level: 40 },
    { name: 'Lung', window: 1500, level: -600 }
  ]
}
3. Anatomy Labels for CT
javascriptanatomyData.ct = [
  { name: "Skull Base", function: "Bony structures", intensity: "Bone window" },
  { name: "Paranasal Sinuses", function: "Air-filled cavities", intensity: "Soft tissue window" },
  // ... complete anatomical progression
]
Adding Ultrasound Modality
1. Special Considerations

Cine loops (video) vs static images
Different probe types (linear, curved, phased array)
Doppler overlays (color, power, spectral)

2. Implementation Approach
javascriptultrasound: {
  name: 'Ultrasound',
  fullName: 'Ultrasound Imaging',
  imagePath: '/ultrasound/patient_001/',
  probeType: 'Curved Array',
  frequency: '3.5 MHz',
  depth: '15 cm',
  gain: 'Auto',
  hasColorDoppler: true,
  hasCineLoop: true,
  frameRate: 30 // if video
}
Adding PET Modality
1. Special Requirements

Color mapping (rainbow, hot metal, etc.)
SUV (Standardized Uptake Value) measurements
Often fused with CT (PET/CT)

2. Implementation
javascriptpet: {
  name: 'PET',
  fullName: 'Positron Emission Tomography',
  imagePath: '/pet/patient_001/',
  tracer: 'FDG',
  injectedDose: '10 mCi',
  uptakeTime: '60 min',
  colormap: 'rainbow',
  suvMax: 15,
  fusionModality: 'CT' // if PET/CT
}
Implementation Checklist for New Modalities
Pre-Development

 Acquire appropriate dataset with proper licensing
 Verify HIPAA compliance and anonymization
 Determine optimal image format and compression
 Plan directory structure
 Document acquisition parameters

Development

 Update modalityConfig object
 Add anatomical labels to anatomyData
 Implement modality-specific controls (window/level for CT, gain for US)
 Add image loading logic
 Test navigation and controls
 Verify responsive behavior

Post-Development

 Update attribution and citations
 Add educational disclaimers
 Document known limitations
 Create user guide
 Performance testing

Best Practices & Lessons Learned
Image Optimization

WebP format provides best compression for medical images
Lazy loading for large datasets (implement for CT with 200+ slices)
Progressive loading for better UX

User Experience

Consistent controls across modalities
Keyboard shortcuts for power users
Touch gestures for mobile devices
Presets for common viewing scenarios

Medical Accuracy

Always indicate imaging parameters
Show anatomical orientation markers
Include scale/measurement tools
Maintain DICOM-compliant terminology

